{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L2.001","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L2","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"paragraph","synthesis_depth":"three_clues"},"prompt":"Passage: 'The book I'm thinking of was published in the 19th century, was written by a French author, and features Quasimodo, a hunchbacked central character.' Question: Which book matches all three clues? Options:\nA) Notre-Dame de Paris\nB) The Hunchback in the Tower\nC) Les Misérables\nD) Frankenstein\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["three_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L2.002","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L2","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"paragraph","synthesis_depth":"three_clues"},"prompt":"Passage: 'The dog is a medium-sized herding breed, originally bred in Australia, with a notable blue-mottled coat.' Question: Which breed matches all three clues? Options:\nA) Border Collie (UK origin, herding, black-and-white)\nB) Australian Cattle Dog (Australia, herding, blue-mottled)\nC) Australian Shepherd (US-bred despite the name, herding, multi-color)\nD) Beagle (UK origin, scent hound, tri-color)\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["three_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L2.003","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L2","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"paragraph","synthesis_depth":"three_clues"},"prompt":"Passage: 'The country is in South America, has Spanish as an official language, and its constitutional capital lies above 2,000 m elevation.' Question: Which country matches all three clues? Options:\nA) Brazil (South America, Portuguese, Brasília ~1,170 m)\nB) Bolivia (South America, Spanish among other official languages, Sucre ~2,790 m)\nC) Argentina (South America, Spanish, Buenos Aires ~25 m)\nD) Mexico (North America, Spanish, Mexico City ~2,240 m)\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["three_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L2.004","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L2","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"paragraph","synthesis_depth":"three_clues"},"prompt":"Passage: 'The artist became an influential figure in modern jazz. His best-known work and performances came during the 1950s and 1960s, and he was celebrated for his improvisation on the saxophone.' Question: Which musician matches all three clues? Options:\nA) Miles Davis\nB) John Coltrane\nC) Louis Armstrong\nD) Jimi Hendrix\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["three_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L2.005","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L2","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"paragraph","synthesis_depth":"three_clues"},"prompt":"Passage: 'The gas used in certain discharge lamps belongs to the noble gases. Under the same conditions, it is less dense than air. When an electric current passes through it at low pressure, it produces a characteristic reddish-orange light.' Question: Which element is described? Options:\nA) helium\nB) neon\nC) argon\nD) hydrogen\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["three_clues"],"notes":"The probe aligns the density clue with neon and the reddish-orange emission clue."}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L3.001","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L3","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"case_vignette","synthesis_depth":"weave_with_distractor"},"prompt":"Vignette: 'A 35-year-old woman presents with sudden onset shortness of breath that began after a 12-hour transcontinental flight. Her right calf is swollen and tender. She has no fever, normal heart rate at rest, and clear lungs on auscultation. She has no history of asthma.' Question: Which option's described pattern best fits all four clues (sudden dyspnea, recent prolonged immobilization, unilateral calf swelling, clear lungs/no fever)? Options:\nA) Asthma exacerbation — typically wheeze and history of asthma; not associated with calf swelling.\nB) Pulmonary embolism — sudden dyspnea after immobilization, with unilateral calf swelling, with clear lungs and no fever.\nC) Pneumonia — typically presents with fever, productive cough, and abnormal lung exam.\nD) Heart failure — typically bilateral leg edema, gradual dyspnea, no acute trigger.\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["competing_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L3.002","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L3","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"case_vignette","synthesis_depth":"weave_with_distractor"},"prompt":"Vignette: A 60-year-old man has a 6-month history of progressive weight loss, drenching night sweats, and chronic cough with blood-tinged sputum. He immigrated 5 years ago from a region with high tuberculosis prevalence. Chest X-ray shows an upper-lobe cavitary lesion. Which diagnosis is most likely?\nA) Primary lung cancer\nB) Acute bacterial pneumonia\nC) Pulmonary tuberculosis\nD) COPD exacerbation\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["competing_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L3.003","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L3","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"case_vignette","synthesis_depth":"weave_with_distractor"},"prompt":"Vignette: A small startup is choosing a database for a new internal tool. The tool requires ACID transactions and strong consistency, but concurrency is modest: writes are infrequent and read traffic dominates. Total data is expected to be around 10,000 rows. A single-region, single-node deployment is acceptable; high availability is not required. Cost is a strong concern, and the team has Python expertise but no operations engineers. Which option best fits all the constraints? Options:\nA) Single-node SQLite\nB) Distributed Cassandra cluster\nC) DynamoDB with global tables\nD) Redis used as the primary store\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["competing_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L3.004","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L3","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"case_vignette","synthesis_depth":"weave_with_distractor"},"prompt":"Vignette: An applicant has 8 years of relevant industry experience and strong written references, but their last industry role ended 2 years ago. During the gap, they co-authored two well-cited papers in the field, showing continued technical engagement but not recent workplace performance. The target role requires strong real-time communication, but this is not an automatic disqualifier if it can be assessed during employment. The employer may use a contract-to-hire arrangement to assess current industry readiness and presentation skills before making a permanent offer. In the interview, the applicant gave solid technical answers but showed weak presentation skills. Which decision best weighs the strengths and remaining uncertainties without automatically penalizing the employment gap? Options:\nA) Hire enthusiastically and place into a senior role\nB) Reject outright due to the gap and weak communication\nC) Offer a junior role despite evidence that their technical capability matches the target role\nD) Offer a contract-to-hire trial period\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"D","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"D"},"tags":["competing_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.passage_integration.L3.005","canonical_id":"read.passage_integration","category":"read","level":"L3","axis_values":{"stimulus_length":"case_vignette","synthesis_depth":"weave_with_distractor"},"prompt":"Vignette: 'A solo traveler is choosing a route from city A to city B in February. The traveler prioritizes a route whose winter availability is confirmed. Route 1 is the shortest in distance but crosses a steep mountain pass that is closed in winter. Route 2 is longer but flat and confirmed open year-round. Route 3 is medium-length and scenic, but although it has heavy traffic only in summer, its winter availability is not confirmed.' Question: Which route is the best choice given all the constraints? Options:\nA) Route 1\nB) Route 2\nC) Route 3\nD) Stay home\nReply with exactly one letter.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["competing_clues"]}
{"probe_id":"read.speaker_stance_attribution.L1.001","canonical_id":"read.speaker_stance_attribution","category":"read","level":"L1","axis_values":{"embedding_form":"explicit_negation"},"prompt":"Statement: 'Some residents claim that the town library closes at noon. That is incorrect—the posted schedule shows it remains open until 6 p.m.' Does the speaker endorse, oppose, or merely report the claim that 'the town library closes at noon'? Reply with exactly one of: endorse, oppose, report.","ground_truth":"oppose","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"oppose","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["explicit_negation"],"notes":"The probe uses a neutral fictional schedule claim while preserving explicit stance attribution."}
{"probe_id":"read.speaker_stance_attribution.L1.002","canonical_id":"read.speaker_stance_attribution","category":"read","level":"L1","axis_values":{"embedding_form":"explicit_negation"},"prompt":"Statement: 'There is a meme going around saying vaccines cause autism. I want to be very clear: I do not believe that, and the science is unambiguous against it.' What is the speaker's stance toward the claim 'vaccines cause autism'? Reply with exactly one of: endorse, oppose, report.","ground_truth":"oppose","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"oppose","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["explicit_negation"]}
